window.__saqPaperQuestions("2019-2-northern-sydney",[{"number":1,"title":"","stemText":"A 33yo male is brought to ED following a suicide attempt with ingestion of an unknown\nsubstance 4hrs prior to presentation.\n\nVital signs:\n\nHR 105\n\nBP 142/79\n\nRR 30\n\nSaO2 94% 6L HMO2\n\nGCS E2V3M4 = 9\nAn urgent ABG and bloods are collected with the following results:\n\npH\n7.02\n\npCO2\n\npO2\n\nHCO3\n\nLactate\n8.2\n\nGlucose\n\nUrea\n\nCreatinine\n210 Osmolarity 299\n\nNa\n142\n\nK\n4.0\n\nCl\n106 Ca 1.9 Mg 0.8","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Describe the acid-base disturbances (2 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"What 3 other abnormalities are present on the ABG? What is the likely diagnosis and\nwhy? (5 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"List 2 other investigations you would request and your rationale for each. (4 marks)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"The patient goes on to have a generalised tonic-clonic seizure lasting 5mins.\nDescribe 6 steps in your management of this patient. (6 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>RAGMA </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>AG = 142 – (106+8) = 142-114 = 28 </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>Delta ratio = (28-12)/(24-8) = 1 </em>→<em> </em><em><strong>isolated RAGMA </strong></em><em>with elevated lactate </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>Expected PCO2 = (1.5x8)+ 8 = 20 </em>→<em> </em><em><strong>compensatory respiratory alkalosis (appropriate) </strong></em></div><div class=\"answer-line\">ii.</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>1. elevated osmolar gap </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>Calc osm = 2x142 + 24 + 8 = 316 </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>Osmolar gap = 316 – 299 = 17&gt;10 </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>2.  hypocalcaemia </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Practice SAQ Paper Oct 2019</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Northern Sydney Hospitals</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>3.  renal impairment </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>(4. elevated lactate) </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\">→<em> </em><em><strong>ETHYLENE GLYCOL TOXICITY </strong></em></div><div class=\"answer-line\">-</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em><strong>RAGMA with elevated lactate and elevated osmolar gap, associated with </strong></em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em><strong>hypcalcaemia and rising creatinine = pathognomonic of EG toxicity </strong></em></div><div class=\"answer-line\">iii.</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><thead><tr><th>Investigation</th><th>Justification</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><em>Blood alcohol level - ?co-ingestion </em></td><td><em>Serum ethylene glycol level – to determine need for haemodialysis (&gt;8mmol/L) though </em></td></tr><tr><td><em>usually not readily available and decision to haemodialyse based on other factors </em></td><td><em>Urine microscopy - ?Ca oxalate crystals in urine </em></td></tr><tr><td>ii.</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><em>Transfer to RR and attach non-invasive monitoring </em></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><em>IV diazepam (5-10mg) for termination of seizure </em></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><em>RSI/intubation for airway protection with seizure and poor GCS: maintain hyperventilation to </em></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><em>continue respiratory compensation </em></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><em>Administer antidote: ethanol IV 8ml/kg loading 10% ethanol, continue 1-2ml/kg/h </em></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><em>Correct hypocalcaemia: CaCl 10ml IV over 5mins </em></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><em>IV NaHCO3 1-2mmol/kg for correction of acidosis </em></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><em>Arrange for haemodialysis – indications:  </em></td></tr><tr><td>•</td><td><em>large EG ingestion with osmolar gap&gt;10 </em></td></tr><tr><td>•</td><td><em>pH&lt;7.3 </em></td></tr><tr><td>•</td><td><em>acute renal failure </em></td></tr><tr><td>•</td><td><em>EG level &gt;8mmol/L </em></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><em>Admit patient to ICU for monitoring and haemodialysis treatment </em></td></tr><tr><td>Practice SAQ Paper Oct 2019</td><td>Northern Sydney Hospitals</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":2,"title":"","stemText":"You are the consultant on duty in an urban district ED. You are called to triage where an angry father is shouting ‘this hospital missed that my son has a broken hand ’. The father is accompanied by a 12 year old son, Max, who carries a letter from GP stating that the GP has looked up the x-ray report for Max, who was seen in your ED 3 days prior with hand pain after a fall. The x-ray report states there is a displaced scaphoid fracture.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"How do you initially manage this situation at triage? (2 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"What are your immediate clinical priorities? (3 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"Max’s father puts in a formal complaint about Max’s care during his initial presentation to ED. What are your next steps in terms of investigating this matter further? (5 marks)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"You wish to develop a policy to formalise the radiology results checking process for the department. What are the main features of a policy document? (4 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Introduce yourself/ Identify yourself as senior staff member</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Aim to verbally de-escalate the Father and offer to take him to a private area to</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">talk</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Acknowledge his distress. Reassure him that you would like to hear more about</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">the case/ take his concerns seriously</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>arrange immediate treatment for Max</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Anything sensible that involves acknowledging problem/ de-escalation</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Must not ‘blame’ anybody, or offer explanation without investigation first.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Need to be<strong> clinical</strong> interventions</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td><strong>Analgesia </strong></td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td><strong>Immobilisation </strong></td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>+/- repeat imaging</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td><strong>Orthopaedics / hands consult </strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Max’s father puts in a formal complaint about Max’s care during his initial</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">presentation to ED. What are your next steps in terms of investigating this</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">matter further? (5 marks)</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Formally acknowledge the complaint/ incident in writing with an expected time</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">frame for how long the review process will take</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Inform patient liaison/ complaints and the quality department</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td><strong>Review the medical notes/ documentation </strong></td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Interview the staff involved and support them</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td><strong>Review the department’s results checking processes / any departmental </strong></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><strong>guidelines or policies relating to the presentation  </strong></td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Formalise your findings and any recommendations in written format and write</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">any actions arising from this (eg review a change in policy etc)</td></tr><tr><td>Practice SAQ Paper Oct 2019</td><td>Northern Sydney Hospitals</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Present your findings in department M&amp;M</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td><strong>Provide education to department/ the staff involved</strong> (individual education or as</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">a department, eg formal teaching sessions)</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td><strong>Feedback your findings to patient / patient’s family, </strong>with an apology, if</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">appropriate</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"2\">You wish to develop a policy to formalise the radiology results checking process</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">for the department. What are the main features of a policy document? (4 marks)</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td><strong>Statement of intent – why/ what </strong></td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td><strong>Purpose – for whom/ rationale </strong></td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Scope – who or what is affected</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td><strong>Expected outcomes </strong></td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Related policies/ references</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Outline the expected procedure or recommendation – <strong>clearly list the steps </strong></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><strong>involved</strong></td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>What documentation is expected for this procedure</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Any associated education/ training</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Who authorises/ approves this policy</td></tr><tr><td>Practice SAQ Paper Oct 2019</td><td>Northern Sydney Hospitals</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":3,"title":"","stemText":"You have observed a trainee perform a physical exam on a 70-year-old gentleman who had a syncope walking up stairs. He has been breathless on exertion for 1 year. Your trainee finds no abnormality on examination and considers that he is suitable for discharge if baseline blood tests, 12 lead ECG and a period of monitoring do not reveal any abnormality. You decide to examine him yourself. You find a mid-systolic murmur, that radiates to the carotids and crepitations (crackles) to the midzones in both lung fields.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"What is your differential diagnosis of this murmur and the significance of each option? (8 marks) Condition Typical features on auscultation Clinical significance Aortic stenosis Aortic sclerosis Hypertrophic\n• bstructive\ncardiomyopathy Mitral regurgitation"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"What is your advice to the trainee on this patient’s disposition, including your rationale? (2 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"Your advice:"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"Your rationale:"},{"id":"e","label":"Part E","prompt":"Your trainee had asked for this case to be a CEX but now doesn’t want to because they did poorly. You become aware that other consultants have had the same issue regarding underperformance. As the DEMT you decide to meet with the trainee and discuss a performance plan."},{"id":"f","label":"Part F","prompt":"List 4 early warning signs of a trainee in difficulty (2 marks)"},{"id":"g","label":"Part G","prompt":"List the parts of a performance plan. ( 4 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">You have observed a trainee perform a physical exam on a 70-year-old gentleman who had a</div><div class=\"answer-line\">syncope walking up stairs.  He has been breathless on exertion for 1 year.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Your trainee finds no abnormality on examination and considers that he is suitable for discharge if</div><div class=\"answer-line\">baseline blood tests, 12 lead ECG and a period of monitoring do not reveal any abnormality.</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">You decide to examine him yourself. You find a mid-systolic murmur, that radiates to the carotids</div><div class=\"answer-line\">and crepitations (crackles) to the midzones in both lung fields.</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Admit under cardiology for inpatient workup eg TTE-.  (or at the very least needs a</div><div class=\"answer-line\">cardiology consult while in ED and early followup arranged)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Avoid nitrates / diuretics.</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Previously undiagnosed AS most likely, and clinically this would be severe given the syncope,</div><div class=\"answer-line\">dyspnoea and crackles. Needs identification of severity of AS, commencement of treatment</div><div class=\"answer-line\">and monitoring.</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part E</h5><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>++ Disappearing; not answering pagers; lateness; frequent sick leave; </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>++ Slow in performing procedures; </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>++ Reluctant to make decisions; </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>++ Difficulty in prioritising; </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>++ Failure to seek advice appropriately; </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>++ Lack of initiative; </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>++ Fellow trainees and nursing staff avoid seeking the trainee’s opinion or help; </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>++ Not completing WBAs or ITAs; </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>++ Deferring tasks to others, avoiding tasks; </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>++ Lack of insight into limitations</em><em>. </em></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part F</h5><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>1.</em><em> </em><em>Identify the performance area that needs improvement </em></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part G</h5><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>3.</em><em> </em><em>Discuss reasons that could be causing poor performance </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>4.</em><em> </em><em>Create a performance action plan with a set of goals and time frames for next meetings </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>and reviews </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Practice SAQ Paper Oct 2019</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Northern Sydney Hospitals</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":4,"title":"","stemText":"You are the consultant working in a mixed rural ED. A 2 year-old girl is brought in by her mother with a petechial rash on her legs noticed in the past few hours.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Define or describe the difference between “petechiae” and “purpura” (2 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"What are the differentials for non-blanching/ petechial rash in children? (6 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"The child’s observations are: T 36.8, HR 110, RR 26, sats 98% RA, she is alert and acting appropriately for age. What key investigations would you do in ED? (4 marks)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"What criteria would need to be met to safely discharge this child from ED? (3 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Petechiae are small (or “pinpoint”) purpuric lesions &lt; 2mm across</div><div class=\"answer-line\">-</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Purpura are larger non-blanching spots</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"2\">[Must have meningococcal, HSP and ITP]</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Thrombocytopenia</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">o Increased destruction: <strong>ITP, TTP</strong>,</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">o Decreased production: Malignancy (<strong>leukaemia</strong>), aplastic anaemia, drug-induced,</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">radiation therapy, Fanconi anaemia, lympho-proliferative disorders</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">o sequestration</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Platelet dysfunction</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">o Congenital</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">o Acquired: drug/ toxin induced (NSAIDs)</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Coagulation deficiencies</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">o Congenital: Factor VIII deficiency, <strong>von willebrand disease</strong></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">o Acquired: Liver disease, uraemia, vitamin K defieicncy</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Loss of vascular integrity:</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">o Trauma/ Increased venous pressure: <strong>coughing, vomiting, tourniquet, </strong></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><strong>strangulation</strong></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">o Vasculitis: <strong>Henoch Schonlein Purpurae,</strong> SLE</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">o Toxins: penicliins, steroids, sulfonamidses</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">o Sepsis: DIC, HUS, Infection (TORCH, vial, bacterial eg <strong>meningococcal</strong>)</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"2\">The child’s observations are: T 36.8, HR 110, RR 26, sats 98% RA, she is alert and acting</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">appropriately for age. What key investigations would you do in ED? (4 marks)</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>FBC – <strong>platelets/ cell line </strong></td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td><strong>Urinalysis </strong></td></tr><tr><td>Practice SAQ Paper Oct 2019</td><td>Northern Sydney Hospitals</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td><strong>Inflammatory markers (WCC, CRP) </strong></td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Blood pressure</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Consider coagulation</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Normal obs</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Lack of fever</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Low risk sinister cause (CRP &lt; 8, WCC 5- 15 etc), likely non sinister cause</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Period of observation, stable</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Physical Senior review</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Safety nets explained – reasons to represent etc, parents sensible etc</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Appropriate early follow up arranged – paeds/ GP etc</td></tr><tr><td>Practice SAQ Paper Oct 2019</td><td>Northern Sydney Hospitals</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":5,"title":"","stemText":"A 7-year old girl returns to the ED with private films. She was seen four weeks ago with\nelbow pain and diagnosed with a sprain. AN X-RAY IS REPRODUCED IN THE PROPS BOOK","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Describe the x-rays noting relevant negatives (4 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"The doctor involved has had several missed injuries recently and you are concerned about burn out. List five symptoms or signs of burn out (5 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"Shift work is a possible contributor to burn out. Night shifts present specific challenges to well-being. List four personal and four organisational strategies that can be implemented to minimise these difficulties (8 marks) Personal Strategies Organisational Strategies"}],"propPages":["asset:img9fee8b47662e22b5e0"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Right elbow AP and lateral films.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">AP film: <strong>fracture through radial neck with head displaced (&gt;100%)</strong>.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Lateral film: Probable large anterior &amp; small posterior fat pad. Bony lines intact: anterior</div><div class=\"answer-line\">humeral and radiocapitellar lines.</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">The doctor involved has had a number of missed injuries recently and you are</div><div class=\"answer-line\">concerned about burn out. List five symptoms or signs of burn out (5 marks)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">-general exhaustion and fatigue</div><div class=\"answer-line\">-lack of motivation in life</div><div class=\"answer-line\">-cynicism and negativity</div><div class=\"answer-line\">-difficulty concentrating and task completion</div><div class=\"answer-line\">-deterioration in job performance</div><div class=\"answer-line\">-interpersonal problems outside work</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Practice SAQ Paper Oct 2019</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Northern Sydney Hospitals</div><div class=\"answer-line\">-unhealthy lifestyle choices</div><div class=\"answer-line\">-poor job satisfaction</div><div class=\"answer-line\">-constant pre-occupation with work</div><div class=\"answer-line\">-development of health issues eg depression, obesity, HTN</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Shift work is a possible contributor to burn out. Night shifts present specific</div><div class=\"answer-line\">challenges to well-being. List four personal and four organisational strategies that</div><div class=\"answer-line\">can be implemented to minimise these difficulties (8 marks)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Personal Strategies</div><div class=\"answer-line\">-exercise before sleeping</div><div class=\"answer-line\">-outside time with exposure to natural light before sleep</div><div class=\"answer-line\">-well darkened room to sleep during daytime</div><div class=\"answer-line\">-eating healthy food, avoiding junk food</div><div class=\"answer-line\">-melatonin to help reset Circadian rhythm</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Organisational strategies</div><div class=\"answer-line\">-adequate staffing for the ED overnight</div><div class=\"answer-line\">-rostered senior staff oncall</div><div class=\"answer-line\">-adequate bed flow without access block, no boarding in ED</div><div class=\"answer-line\">-rostering in shorter blocks eg 4 nights not 7 in a row.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">-days off to recover after nights</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Practice SAQ Paper Oct 2019</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Northern Sydney Hospitals</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":6,"title":"","stemText":"A 25yo male is brought to ED following assault with a cricket bat one hour ago. He had a witnessed\nloss of consciousness for 5mins at the scene, with current GCS 14 (E4V4M6).","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Aside from intracranial haematoma, list three possible early complications of head trauma, and give two signs that would suggest the presence of each complication: (6 marks) Complication Sign"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List the five high risk and the two medium risk features of the Canadian CT Head Rule (5 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"If a significant traumatic brain injury is detected in this patient, what measures should be taken to prevent secondary brain injury? (5 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Aside from intracranial haematoma, list three possible early complications of head trauma, and</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">give two signs that would suggest the presence of each complication: (6 marks)</td></tr><tr><td>Complication</td><td>Sign</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><em>anisocoria </em></td></tr><tr><td><em>Increased ICP </em></td><td><em>altered mental state </em></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><em>hypertension </em></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><em>bradycardia </em></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><em>papilloedema </em></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><em>decorticate/decerebrate posturing </em></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><em>Battle sign (postauricular haemorrhage) </em></td></tr><tr><td><em>Base of skull fracture </em></td><td><em>haemotympanum </em></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><em>CSF otorrhoea/rhinorrhoea </em></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><em>Subconjunctival haemorrhage </em></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><em>visual loss/defect </em></td></tr><tr><td><em>Cranial nerve injury </em></td><td><em>reduced eye movements </em></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><em>facial asymmetry </em></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><em>Others: </em></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><em>Contusions – decreased GCS, seizure </em></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><em>Aspiration pneumonia – reduced air entry, hypoxia, tachycardia, fever, tachypnoea </em></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><em>Neurogenic cardiac dysfunction </em></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><em>Neurogenic pulmonary oedema – hypoxia, crepitations, tachypnoea </em></td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>Canadian CT rules: </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>HIGH RISK </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>- GCS &lt;15 at 2h after injury </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>- suspected open or depressed skull # </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>- any sign of basal skull #: haemotympanum, &#x27;racoon&#x27; eyes, CSF otorrohoea/rhinorrhoea, Battle&#x27;s </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>sign </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>- vomiting &gt; or +2 episodes </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>- age &gt;65yo </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>MEDIUM RISK </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Practice SAQ Paper Oct 2019</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Northern Sydney Hospitals</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>- amnesia before impact &gt;30min </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>- dangerous mechanism (pedestrian v. MVA, ejection from vehicle, fall from height &gt;3ft or 5 </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>stairs) </em></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"2\">If a significant traumatic brain injury is detected in this patient, what measures should be taken to</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">prevent secondary brain injury? (5 marks)</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Maintaining cerebral oxygen supply:</td></tr><tr><td>•</td><td><strong>Normoxia</strong>: keep the PaO2 above 60 mmHg</td></tr><tr><td>•</td><td>Low <strong>normocapnia</strong>: keep the PaCO2 between 35-40 mmHg</td></tr><tr><td>•</td><td><strong>Normotension</strong>: measure the MAP, and keep the systolic above 90mmHg</td></tr><tr><td>•</td><td>Intracranial Pressure monitoring: keep it under 20mmHg</td></tr><tr><td>•</td><td>Cerebral perfusion pressure: keep it 50-70mmHg</td></tr><tr><td>•</td><td>Cerebral oxygenation monitoring:keep the SjO2 &gt;50%, and PbrO2 &gt;55mmHg</td></tr><tr><td>•</td><td>Managing increased intracranial pressure for which there is a variety of strategies:</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">o Draining the EVD ( about 20ml/hr, max)</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">o <strong>Head up 30 degrees </strong></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">o Positioning the head straight</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">o <strong>Removing the C-spine collar </strong></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">o Sedation :</td></tr><tr><td>▪</td><td>Propofol sedation to decrease distress and thus decrease ICP</td></tr><tr><td>▪</td><td>Barbiturate coma if other methods of lowering ICP have failed</td></tr><tr><td>▪</td><td>Analgesia to prevent increased ICP in response to suctioning and routine care</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">o Paralysis</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">o Osmotherapy</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">o Controversial measures</td></tr><tr><td>▪</td><td>Decompressive craniectomy</td></tr><tr><td>▪</td><td>Hypothermia</td></tr><tr><td>▪</td><td>Dexamethasone</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Decreasing cerebral oxygen demand:</td></tr><tr><td>•</td><td>Sedation</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">o Propofol sedation to decrease distress and thus decrease ICP</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">o Barbiturate coma if medical and surgical methods of lowering ICP have failed</td></tr><tr><td>•</td><td>Analgesia - opioid selection is irrelevant, but opiate boluses increase ICP</td></tr><tr><td>•</td><td><strong>Seizure prophylaxis</strong> is infrequently indicated, and the course is 7 days only</td></tr><tr><td>Practice SAQ Paper Oct 2019</td><td>Northern Sydney Hospitals</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":7,"title":"","stemText":"A 36 hour old neonate presents with a community nurse because of failure to pass\nmeconium. There has been mucous-like vomiting but the neonate otherwise looks well.\n\nPR, BP, RR and saturations are within normal ranges.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"With regard to this presentation, (4 marks) (a) A normal digital rectal examination excludes which diagnosis? (b) The successful passage of an NG tube excludes which diagnosis? (c) What is the characteristic X-ray appearance of duodenal atresia? (d) Name a congenital condition associated with neonatal bowel obstruction."},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"Prior to transport to a specialist paediatric centre name 5 essential actions you should perform in ED to ensure the safety of the neonate in transfer (5 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"Describe the intravenous maintenance fluid choice and maintenance fluid rate you would prescribe for this neonate prior to transfer. (2 marks)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"Name 2 other potential causes of neonatal intestinal obstruction at this age not\nnamed in Question (i) (2 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">A 36 hour old neonate presents with a community nurse because of failure to pass</div><div class=\"answer-line\">meconium.   There has been mucous-like vomiting but the neonate otherwise looks well.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">PR, BP, RR and saturations are within normal ranges.</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Prior to transport to a specialist paediatric centre name 5 essential actions you</div><div class=\"answer-line\">should perform in ED to ensure the safety of the neonate in transfer  (5 marks)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Vital signs documentation</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Oxygen as required to maintain saturations</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Temperature measurement and balance</div><div class=\"answer-line\">BSL measurement and correction if &lt; 3mmol/L</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Establish iv access and place nbm</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Establish iv fluid maintenance</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Place a ng tube on free drainage</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Abdominal X-ray</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Describe the intravenous maintenance fluid choice and maintenance fluid rate</div><div class=\"answer-line\">you would prescribe for this neonate prior to transfer.  (2 marks)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Fluid;  N/2 +10% dextrose or N/4 + 10% dex</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Rate 4ml/kg/hr; might state 3.75-5ml/kg/hr or 90-120ml/kg/day<span class=\"answer-source-red\"> </span></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Name 2 other potential causes of neonatal intestinal obstruction at this age not <span class=\"answer-source-red\"><em> </em></span></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Enteric duplication cysts, Gastric volvulus, Gastric atresia, Microgastria, Duodenal web</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Malrotion. Jejunal stenosis and atresia.  Small bowel obs.  Ileal atresia Intestinal mal</div><div class=\"answer-line\">rotation.  Meconium ileus, meconium plug Small left colon syndrome.  Intussusception.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Intestinal atresia, Colonic atresia, Hirshprungs.  Anal atresia, Anorectal abnormalities</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Practice SAQ Paper Oct 2019</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Northern Sydney Hospitals</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":8,"title":"","stemText":"A 48 year old female presents to your emergency department with 24 hours of mild chest\ndiscomfort and exertional dyspnoea. She is normally well without significant past history or regular medications. She occasionally smokes cigarettes\nHer observations at triage are\nPulse 80 bpm\n\nBP 137/70 mmHg\nAfebrile\n\nRR 22/minute\n\nSats 94%\nAN X-RAY IS REPRODUCED IN THE PROPS BOOK","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Describe and interpret her PA CXR (2 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"In the following table list 2 historical features and their implications for your management decisions (4 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"Describe a radiological feature that may influence your management (2 marks)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"List and justify 2 possible management strategies (4 marks)"},{"id":"e","label":"Part E","prompt":"You decide she can be discharged based on your assessment and management. What discharge advice would you provide to her? (4 marks)"}],"propPages":["asset:img7f3f7d3d3d2d3d3ec9"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\"><strong>Left </strong>sided <strong>pneumothorax. </strong> BTS guideline moderate to large 1-2cm interpleural measured at</div><div class=\"answer-line\">the hilum</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Practice SAQ Paper Oct 2019</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Northern Sydney Hospitals</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">In the following table list 2 historical features and their implications for your</div><div class=\"answer-line\">management decisions  (4 marks)</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Large vs. small. Could also have midline shift/radiological ?tension</div><div class=\"answer-line\">BTS vs. ATS rules e.g. &gt;2cm interpleural = large → More likely to require intervention.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Something sensible</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Conservative. Minimal symptomatology at rest. 1st Primary pneumothorax suspected</div><div class=\"answer-line\">without obvious underlying structural abnormality on CXR. Patient preference etc.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Progress film e.g. 6-12 hours for stability. Follow up progress film 2 weeks</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Simple aspiration 16-18 G needle &lt;2.5 litres</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Faster resolution of symptoms/size in primary spontaneous</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Pt. preference</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Early discharge</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Practice SAQ Paper Oct 2019</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Northern Sydney Hospitals</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Chest drain e.g. pig tail. 8-14F</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Secondary pneumothorax suspected (recurrence/underlying lung disease)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Significant symptoms</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Chest discomfort not controlled with simple analgesics (sensible)</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part E</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">You decide she can be discharged based on your assessment and management.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">What discharge advice would you provide to her? (4 marks)</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>Follow up</em>  – LMO vs. resp physician and timing.   Progress film e.g. 2-4 weeks.</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>High risk activity advice </em>(1)<em>. </em>No flying until full resolution/clearance by resp physician</div><div class=\"answer-line\">(sensible)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">No diving at any point (unless surgical fixation and normal CT with clearance)</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>Return advice </em>-<em> Recurrence</em>/worsening of symptoms e.g. SOB/chest pain</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>Cessation of smoking </em>- Likely single most important causal factor <em> </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Practice SAQ Paper Oct 2019</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Northern Sydney Hospitals</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":9,"title":"","stemText":"A 29 year old female is brought to your emergency department by ambulance with a sudden\n• nset of severe shortness of breath one hour prior to presentation. She is previously well\nand her only medications are the oral contraceptive pill. She has no allergies.\n\nVital Signs\n• Temperature - 36.5\n• Pulse rate -130 bpm\n• Blood pressure - 80/ 60 mmHg\n• Saturations - 82% on room air\n• Respiratory rate -55 bpm\nShe is cyanosed and talking in short sentences only","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List three differential diagnoses in order of probability (3 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List 4 possible indications for thrombolysis in patients with pulmonary embolus.\nIndicate the one most clearly shown to decrease mortality (5 Marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"List 3 diagnostic tests for pulmonary embolism along with a description of what a\npositive result would look like for each test. (6 marks) Test Positive result"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"Indicate which test you would use to make the decision to thrombolyse in this case and why (2 marks)"},{"id":"e","label":"Part E","prompt":"List 2 contraindications to thrombolysis for pulmonary embolism. Indicate if they are\nabsolute or relative (2 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td><em>1.</em><em> </em></td><td><em>PE (first) </em></td></tr><tr><td><em>2.</em><em> </em></td><td><em>Cardiogenic shock due to things like myocardial infarction or dissection with tamponade </em></td></tr><tr><td><em>3.</em><em> </em></td><td><em>Severe sepsis with rapid progression as in meningococcal or staph pneumonia </em></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">4. <em>Poisoning with something that causes respiratory or cardiac compromise very quickly</em></td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">List 4 possible indications for thrombolysis in patients with pulmonary embolus.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Indicate the one most clearly shown to decrease mortality (5 Marks)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">● Shock (BP &lt;90 systolic or a drop of 40 from baseline with signs of shock)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">● Severe or worsening right ventricular dysfunction (&quot;submassive PE&quot;)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">● Cardiopulmonary arrest due to PE (eg, BP &gt;90 mmHg after resuscitation)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">● Extensive clot burden (eg, large perfusion defects on ventilation/perfusion scan</div><div class=\"answer-line\">or extensive embolic burden on computed tomography)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">● Free-floating right atrial or ventricular thrombus</div><div class=\"answer-line\">● Severe hypoxia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">● Worsening despite anticoagulation</div><div class=\"answer-line\">(cf up to date accessed Sept 2018)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Practice SAQ Paper Oct 2019</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Northern Sydney Hospitals</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Response / justification</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>List 3 diagnostic tests for pulmonary embolism along with a description of what</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>a positive result would look like for each test. (6 marks)</td><td></td></tr><tr><td><em>Test </em></td><td><em>Positive result </em></td></tr><tr><td><em>Bedside echo</em></td><td><em><strong>Severe RV dilatation +/- compression of LV</strong></em><em>. </em></td></tr><tr><td><em>(thrombus in RA or RV, absence of RV hypertrophy, dilated IVC, TVR </em></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><em>showing high RV pressures)</em></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><em>CTPA</em></td><td><em>Filling defects in major pulmonary vessels</em></td></tr><tr><td><em>V/Q</em></td><td><em>Unmatched perfusion defects</em></td></tr><tr><td><em>D Dimer</em></td><td><em>Positive &gt; 0.5</em></td></tr><tr><td><em>Pulmonary </em></td><td><em>catheter through IV</em></td></tr><tr><td><em>angiogram</em></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><em>MRI pulmonary </em></td><td><em>Ok for picking big PEs in major vessels may be an option for pregnant </em></td></tr><tr><td><em>angiogram</em></td><td><em>women (but not in this case- too unstable) </em></td></tr><tr><td><em>Simultaneous T wave inversions in the inferior (II, III, aVF) and right </em></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>ECG</td><td></td></tr><tr><td><em>precordial leads (V1-4) is the most specific finding (99% in one study)  </em></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><em>Sinus tachycardia</em><em><strong> –</strong></em><em> the most common abnormality; seen in 44% of </em></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><em>patients </em></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><em>Lots of other abnormalities </em></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><em>Complete or incomplete RBBB</em><em><strong> –</strong></em><em> associated with increased mortality; </em></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><em>seen in 18% of patients. </em></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><em>Right ventricular strain pattern</em><em><strong> –</strong></em><em> T wave inversions in the right </em></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><em>precordial leads (V1-4) ± the inferior leads (II, III, aVF). This pattern is </em></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><em>seen in up to 34% of patients and is associated with high pulmonary </em></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><em>artery pressures. </em></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Practice SAQ Paper Oct 2019</td><td>Northern Sydney Hospitals</td></tr><tr><td><em>Right axis deviation – seen in 16% of patients. Extreme right axis </em></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><em>deviation may occur, with axis between zero and -90 degrees, giving </em></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><em>the appearance of left axis deviation (“pseudo left axis”). </em></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><em>Dominant R wave in V1</em><em><strong> </strong></em><em>– a manifestation of acute right ventricular </em></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><em>dilatation. </em></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><em>Right atrial enlargement (P pulmonale)</em><em><strong> – </strong></em><em>peaked P wave in lead II &gt; </em></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><em>2.5 mm in height. Seen in 9% of patients. </em></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><em><strong>SI QIII TIII  pattern</strong></em><em> – deep S wave in lead I, Q wave in III, inverted T </em></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><em>wave in III. This “classic” finding is neither sensitive nor specific for </em></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><em>pulmonary embolism; found in only 20% of patients with PE. </em></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><em>Clockwise rotation</em><em><strong> – </strong></em><em>shift of the R/S transition point towards V6 with </em></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><em>a persistent S wave in V6 (“pulmonary disease pattern”), implying </em></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><em>rotation of the heart due to right ventricular dilatation</em></td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Indicate which test you would use to make the decision to thrombolyse <em>in this </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>case </em>and why (2 marks)</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>The test could be any of the above apart from ddimer (too slow and non specific) and MRI </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>and v/q (too slow) </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>The reason why should indicate the need for speed and some specificity.  </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>They can say it depends on what is available at their place of work.  </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>For echo and ecg the benefits of not having to move the patients </em></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part E</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">List 2 contraindications to thrombolysis for pulmonary embolism. Indicate if</div><div class=\"answer-line\">they are absolute or relative (2 marks)</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em><strong>Absolute contraindications   </strong></em></div><div class=\"answer-line\">● <em>Previous intracranial haemorrhage  </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Practice SAQ Paper Oct 2019</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Northern Sydney Hospitals</div><div class=\"answer-line\">● <em> Structural intracranial disease   </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\">● <em>Ischaemic stroke within 3 months  </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\">● <em> Recent brain or spinal surgery   </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\">● <em>Recent head trauma with fracture or brain injury   </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\">● <em>Active bleeding  </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em><strong>Relative contraindications </strong></em><em>  </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\">● <em>Traumatic cardiopulmonary resuscitation &lt;10 days </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\">● <em> Recent bleeding: major &lt;6 months; minor &lt;10 days if in non-compressible site   </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\">● <em>Recent surgery (&lt; 3 months) or invasive procedure   </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\">● <em>Ischaemic stroke &gt; 3 months previously   </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\">● <em>GI ulceration or varices in last 3 months   </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\">● <em>Malignancy with bleeding risk   </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\">● <em>Anticoagulation prior to presentation   </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\">● <em>Diabetic retinopathy   </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\">● <em>Pregnancy   </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\">● <em>Endocarditis, pericarditis or pericardial effusion   </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\">● <em>Acute pancreatitis, </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\">● <em>Age &gt;75  Low body weight &lt;60kg   </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\">● <em>Systolic &gt;180mmHg or Diastolic &gt;110mmHg  </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>1. Kearon, C., Akl, E.A., Comerota, A.J. et al. Antithrombotic therapy for VTE disease. </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>Antithrombotic therapy and Prevention of thrombosis, 9th ed. American College of Chest </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>Physicians. Chest 2012; 141 (2) (Suppl): e419s 2. Actilyse (Alteplase) Prescribing </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>Information (PI), TGA website</em><strong> </strong></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Practice SAQ Paper Oct 2019</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Northern Sydney Hospitals</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":10,"title":"","stemText":"An 8 year-old boy of Middle Eastern descent is brought into hospital with fevers, chest pain and shortness of breath. He has had previous presentations with swollen, painful joints.\n\nObservations:\n\nTemp 37.9\n\nHR 110\n\nBP 100/60\n\nSats 93% RA\n\nRR 28\nOn examination he has crackles in the right mid zone. His abdomen is soft and non tender. He has mild splenomegaly. He has no joint or long bone pain.\n\nBlood Tests:\n\nHb 74\nMCV 85\n\nWCC 14 (normal neutrophils)\n\nPlatelets 264\nReticuloytes 10%\nLDH 822\nBlood cultures have been sent. Other blood tests are unremarkable. A CXR shows R mid zone changes.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Give 5 important findings on the blood results and explain their overall significance\n(5 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"What is the most likely diagnosis (1 mark)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"List 3 potential triggers for this diagnosis (3 marks)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"Outline and justify your management (5 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Give 5 important findings on the blood results and explain their overall significance</div><div class=\"answer-line\">(5 marks)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Normocytic anaemia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Elevated reticulocyte count and elevated LDH levels</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Normal WCC and platelets so not pancytopaenic</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Overall picture suggestive of haemolytic process causing normocytic anaemia</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Sickle cell anaemia with acute chest syndrome</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Infection, Dehydration, Acidosis, Spontaneous</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">20mls/kg IV 0.9% NaCl  - correct any dehydration to decrease viscosity</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Titrated O2 to maintain Sats above 95% - hypoxia can precipitate crisis</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Titrated IV morphine/fentanyl to pain + simple analgesics</div><div class=\"answer-line\">IV ceftriaxone (50mg/kg) and azithromycin- likely functionally asplenic</div><div class=\"answer-line\">DW Paeds Haem +/- PICU</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Answer guide  book 2 Practice SAQ Paper 2019.2</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Northern Sydney Hospitals</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":11,"title":"","stemText":"You are the consultant on shift in a tertiary centre. A 19-year-old girl is brought into ED by a friend complaining of severe right iliac fossa pain. She has had the pain intermittently for a day or so, and it has become suddenly much worse over the past few hours. She has no significant surgical history. Her obs are Temp 37.7, HR 100, BP 105/70, sats 97% RA, RR 18,\n\nBSL 5.6","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"What are four of the most serious differentials to consider in this lady? (4 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List 5 of the components of the Alvarado Score? (5 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"What score in this system is typically used as a “rule out” cut-off with a sensitivity of\naround 96%? (1 mark)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"Her investigations are equivocal for a definitive diagnosis and her repeat\n• bservations are Temp 37.4, HR 90, BP 107/90. She remains in considerable pain\ndespite adequate analgesia in ED. Both the surgical and gynaecology registrars have reviewed the patient and refused to admit her under their care. How do you proceed? (4 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"2\">(Need at least 3 of bolded)</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td><strong>Appendicitis </strong></td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td><strong>Ectopic pregnancy </strong></td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td><strong>Tubo-ovarian abscess/ severe PID </strong>(eg early sepsis from PID)</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Ovarian torsion</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Pyelonephritis/ UTI</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Ruptured ovarian cyst</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>PID (sterile or otherwise)</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>STI</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Accept any other reasonable surgical /gynae differential, but focus on SERIOUS</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">rather than painful</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"2\">RLQ tenderness</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Elevated temperature</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Rebound tenderness</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Migration of pain to the RLQ</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Anorexia</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Nausea or vomiting</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Leukocytosis &gt; 10,000</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Leucocyte left shift</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">What score in this system is typically used as a “rule out” cut-off with a sensitivity of</div><div class=\"answer-line\">around 96%? (1 mark)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">-</div><div class=\"answer-line\">≤ 3</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Her investigations are equivocal for a definitive diagnosis and her repeat</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">observations are Temp 37.4, HR 90, BP 107/90. She remains in considerable pain</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">despite adequate analgesia in ED. Both the surgical and gynaecology registrars have</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">reviewed the patient and refused to admit her under their care. How do you</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">proceed? (4 marks)</td></tr><tr><td>Answer guide  book 2 Practice SAQ Paper 2019.2</td><td>Northern Sydney Hospitals</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Clarify that patient requires admission under inpatient team – undifferentiated</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">abdominal pain, still abnormal obs and requiring ongoing assessment</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Communicate with the respective registrars – eg get both in department</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">together, try to understand their rationale. Try to find compromise eg joint</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">admission, one team accept, the other review daily</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Escalate – team consultants/ medical director</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Demonstrate Patient advocacy</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Consider pushing for OT if unstable/for exploratory lap (with rationale)</td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>Refer to any pre- agreed admission criteria/ policy/ pathway for undifferentiated</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">abdominal if applicable</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">NOT accept – leave in ED/ EDSSU/ send home/ admit under other team eg gen med</td></tr><tr><td>Answer guide  book 2 Practice SAQ Paper 2019.2</td><td>Northern Sydney Hospitals</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":12,"title":"","stemText":"A 23-year-old woman is brought to the ED by her partner who found her at home with a\nfever and shortness of breath. She has been unwell for a few days and using over-the- counter pain tablets. In the ED she is ill-appearing and has RR 30 HR 120 and BP 110/60\n\nTemp 37.9.\nA venous blood gas is performed\n\nNa 137 mmol/L (135-145)\n\nK 3.2 mmol/L (3.5-5.0)\n\nCl 108 mmol/L (100-110)\nBicarb 10 mmol/L (22-27)\n\npH 7.32 (7.35-7.45)\n\npO2 30 (-)\n\npCO2 20 mmHg (35-45)\nBase excess -10 mmol/L (-2 - +2)","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Describe and interpret this venous blood gas demonstrating calculations used where appropriate (6 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"Outline the toxic mechanism of salicylate poisoning (3 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"The patient becomes increasingly drowsy and requires intubation. Explain the main concerns regarding intubation in this context. (2 marks)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"Detail how you would alkalinise the patient’s urine (4 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Describe and interpret this venous blood gas demonstrating calculations used where</div><div class=\"answer-line\">appropriate (6 marks)</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Increased anion gap metabolic acidosis AG 19 mEq/L</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Concomitant normal anion gap metabolic acidosis Delta gap 7.0 mEq/L Delta ratio</div><div class=\"answer-line\">0.5</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Respiratory alkalosis. Expected CO2 = 1.5 x Bicarb + 8 +/- 2 = 21-25</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Consistent with salicylate overdose</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\"> CNS effects dependent on acid/base status – higher penetration across BBB in</div><div class=\"answer-line\">acidaemia (because more salicylate nonionized)</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Central stimulation of respiratory centre  hyperventilation &amp; resp alkalosis.</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Uncoupling of oxidative phosphorylation/Kreb’s cycle alterations  lactic acidosis</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Induces noncardiogenic pulmonary oedema</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Nausea/vomiting by gastric irritations +/- central</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Answer guide  book 2 Practice SAQ Paper 2019.2</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Northern Sydney Hospitals</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">The patient becomes increasingly drowsy and requires intubation. Explain the main</div><div class=\"answer-line\">concerns regarding intubation in this context. (2 marks)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Hyperventilation/respiratory alkalosis must be maintained – iatrogenic acidosis can cause</div><div class=\"answer-line\">rapid increase penetration into CNS and deterioration – seizure, coma, cerebral oedema.</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\"> Important to correct hypokalaemia if present &amp; to maintain normokalaemia (e.g. KCL</div><div class=\"answer-line\">infusion may be required) – tubular H+ exchange for K+</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Give 1-2 mmol/kg sodium bicarb bolus</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Infusion of 100 mmol sodium bicarb in 1000 mL 5% glucose at 250 mL/h</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Administration of glucose important as hypoglycaemia common &amp; detrimental</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Check bicarb &amp; K every 4 h</div><div class=\"answer-line\"> Aim urinary pH &gt; 7.5</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Answer guide  book 2 Practice SAQ Paper 2019.2</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Northern Sydney Hospitals</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":13,"title":"","stemText":"A 72yo female restrained driver is brought into ED following a high-speed MVA in which the vehicle she was travelling in was involved in a head-on collision with severe intrusion to the bonnet of the car. Primary survey:","parts":[{"id":"e","label":"Part E","prompt":"Calculate a Revised Trauma Score for this patient and state the implication of the score. (2 marks)"},{"id":"f","label":"Part F","prompt":"What are the key findings from the x-ray and what is the diagnosis? (4 marks)"},{"id":"g","label":"Part G","prompt":"What other injuries are typically associated with this pathology? (1 mark)"},{"id":"h","label":"Part H","prompt":"List five other investigations you will arrange for this patient, and the rationale for each. (10 marks) Investigation Rationale"}],"propPages":["asset:debug_2019_ns_q13_ct"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"removedNonQuestionResponseFragments":["intact, C-spine collar in situ","equal air entry, midline trachea, SaO2 97% RA, RR 28","HR 102, BP 98/67, CR 2-3s","GCS 15, pupils equal and reactive\nThe patient reports lower back and abdominal pain. AN X-RAY IS REPRODUCED IN THE PROPS BOOK"],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part E</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Calculate a Revised Trauma Score for this patient and state the implication of the</div><div class=\"answer-line\">score. (2 marks)</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>GCS 15 =4, SBP 98 = 4, RR 28 = 4 therefore - RTS = 12 </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Implication- A lower<strong>score</strong> indicates a higher severity of injury.<em> </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Triage- A patient with an RTS score of 12 is labelled delayed, 11 is urgent, and 3–10 is</div><div class=\"answer-line\">immediate. Those who have an RTS below 3 are declared dead and should not receive</div><div class=\"answer-line\">certain care because they are highly unlikely to survive without a significant amount of</div><div class=\"answer-line\">resources</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part F</h5><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>- anterior wedge fracture of L1 vertebral body </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>- horizontal fracture extending into superior posterior aspect of vertebral body </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>- splitting of spinous process </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>- affects all 3 columns, therefore unstable </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em> CHANCE FRACTURE</em></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part G</h5><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>Abdominal injuries (especially pancreas and duodenum) 65% association </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Answer guide  book 2 Practice SAQ Paper 2019.2</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Northern Sydney Hospitals</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part H</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><thead><tr><th>Investigation</th><th>Justification</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>List five other investigations you will arrange for this patient, and the rationale for</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>each. (10 marks)</td><td></td></tr><tr><td><em>FAST ultrasound</em></td><td><em>assessment for intra-abdominal fluid to </em></td></tr><tr><td><em>suggest intra-abdominal injury</em></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><em>CT abdomen</em></td><td><em>abdominal pain and suspicion of intra-</em></td></tr><tr><td><em>abdominal pathology with Chance #</em></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><em>VBG</em></td><td><em>BSL, lactate level, base deficit - ?perfusion, </em></td></tr><tr><td><em>initial measurement to determine adequacy </em></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><em>of fluid resuscitation</em></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><em>FBC</em></td><td><em>Hb level to detect for significant intra-</em></td></tr><tr><td><em>abdominal bleeding</em></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><em>Forensic blood alcohol</em></td><td><em>mandatory for drivers involved in MVA</em></td></tr><tr><td><em>LFT </em></td><td><em>possible hepatic injury </em></td></tr><tr><td><em>Lipase </em></td><td><em>possible pancreatic injury </em></td></tr><tr><td><em>UA </em></td><td><em>?haematuria suggesting renal injury </em></td></tr><tr><td><em>ECG </em></td><td><em>?cause for MVA and underlying cardiac </em></td></tr><tr><td><em>issues </em></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Answer guide  book 2 Practice SAQ Paper 2019.2</td><td>Northern Sydney Hospitals</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":14,"title":"","stemText":"A 35yo female presents to ED with fever and intermittent confusion. She is on the oral\ncontraceptive pill and no other regular medications. Her blood results:\n\nHb\n90g/L MCV 80fL 9.1 x 109/L\n\nWCC\nReticulocytes 4.2%\n22 x 109/L\n\nPlatelets\n\nCreatinine\n180 umol/L","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Complete the table of causes of thrombocytopenia (8 marks): CAUSES DECREASED PRODUCTION INCREASED DESTRUCTION"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"Identify three abnormalities in her blood results and give the most likely diagnosis (4 marks)."},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"Give three additional investigations that you would order and explain the rationale for each (6 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">CAUSES</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Haemolytic anaemia (low Hb, reticulocytosis)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Thrombocytopenia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Renal impairment</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Diagnosis: Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura</div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>This patient has the pentad of: </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>1. fever </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>2. thrombocytopenia </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>3. microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>4. neurological abnormalities </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>5. renal involvement </em></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><thead><tr><th>Investigation</th><th>Justification</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Blood film – for schistocytes/severely fragmented red cells in TTP, identify other RC</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>abnormalities</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>bhCG – pregnancy can precipitate TTP, patient is childbearing age</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Coombs’ test – exclude autoimmune causes, would be expected to be negative in TTP</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Haptoglobin – decreased in haemolytic anaemia</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>LDH – elevated in haemolytic anaemia</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>LFTs – bilirubin level, exclude liver disease as cause of thrombocytopenia</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>UA – haematuria, proteinuria with renal involvement</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Answer guide  book 2 Practice SAQ Paper 2019.2</td><td>Northern Sydney Hospitals</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":15,"title":"","stemText":"A 37-year-old man presents with chest pain following cocaine use.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List and explain three ways in which cocaine use may cause chest pain (6 marks) During the assessment the patient has a brief self-limited seizure. Vital signs: RR 12 bpm P 130 bpm BP 140/69 mmHg SaO2 95% on RA Temp 37 deg C"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"An ECG is performed. Describe and interpret the ECG (2 marks)\nA 12 LEAD ECG IS SHOWN IN THE PROPS BOOKLET"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"Describe two pharmacodynamics properties of cocaine that lead to tachydysrhythmias (2 marks)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"Outline your approach to the treatment of this tachydysrhythmia (3 marks)"},{"id":"e","label":"Part E","prompt":"Beta-selective adrenoreceptor blockers have traditionally been contraindicated in this setting. Why? (2 marks)"}],"propPages":["asset:img3923fa96d24c9ef43c"],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Myocardial ischaemia  Sympathomimetc → hypertension &amp; tachycardia → increased</div><div class=\"answer-line\">myocardial oxygen demand +/- Coronary artery vasospasm +/- Prothrombotic – increased</div><div class=\"answer-line\">platelet aggregation → increase risk of cor a occlusion +/- Chronic use – systemic</div><div class=\"answer-line\">inflammatory state &amp; vessel injury → premature atherosclerosis</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Aortic, coronary (or other vessel) dissection causing pain. Again chronic inflammatory state</div><div class=\"answer-line\">→ vessel injury, weakening of media predisposing to dissection</div><div class=\"answer-line\">“Crack lung” (inflammation-mediated acute pulmonary injury) causing hypoxia, pain,</div><div class=\"answer-line\">infiltrate</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Myocarditis – direct inflammatory effect</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Infective Endocarditis</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Vital signs RR 12 bpm   P 130 bpm   BP 140/69 mmHg   SaO2 95% on RA   Temp 37 deg C</div><div class=\"answer-line\">An ECG is performed. Describe and interpret the ECG (2 marks)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Tachycardia (~ 110 bpm) = probably sinus tachycardia, with P waves embedded in each T</div><div class=\"answer-line\">wave Broad QRS complexes (120 ms, or 3 small squares) Positive R’ wave in lead aVR The</div><div class=\"answer-line\">combination of tachycardia, broad QRS complexes and a positive R’ wave in aVR is strongly</div><div class=\"answer-line\">suggestive of poisoning with a tricyclic antidepressant (or other sodium-channel blocking</div><div class=\"answer-line\">agent)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Answer guide  book 2 Practice SAQ Paper 2019.2</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Northern Sydney Hospitals</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">marks)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Sympathomimetic causing increased heart rate Class I anti-arrhythmic → Na channel</div><div class=\"answer-line\">blockade, widening of QRS and QT prolongation May induce myocardial ischaemia (see</div><div class=\"answer-line\">answers above) leading to dysrhythmias</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Seek &amp; treat electrolyte abnormalities</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Sodium bicarb – counters Na blockade</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Benzodiazepines – reduced sympathetic tone</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part E</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Concerns about unopposed alpha activity causing accelerated hypertension. Controversial</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Answer guide  book 2 Practice SAQ Paper 2019.2</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Northern Sydney Hospitals</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":16,"title":"","stemText":"A 70 year-old female presents to ED with chest pain. The pain radiates to the back, neck and shoulders. She has a hx of hypertension and high cholesterol but is otherwise well. She has been reviewed by the registrar and has normal observations and examination. ECG is normal. The registrar has undertaken bloods and a CXR. Troponin is negative. You are concerned about aortic pathology.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List 4 CXR changes that can be seen in aortic dissection (4 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"The patient has previous severe allergy to iodine contrast. List 3 other investigations which may assist your diagnosis. List tests and a problem associated with each investigation. (6 marks). Test Problem associated with test"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"CT aortogram is undertaken after pre-medication with steroids without complication and the patient remains stable. The test is normal and no clear diagnosis apparent. However, her temperature rises to 38 C and her CRP returns at 200. List 3 differential diagnosis: (3 marks)"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"2\">widened mediastinum: &gt;8.0-8.8 cm at the level of the aortic knob on portable anteroposterior chest</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">radiographs 9,10, although this upper limit of normal varies (may be significantly larger) depending on</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">projection, FFD and x-ray cassette positioning 15</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>double aortic contour</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>irregular aortic contour</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>inward displacement of atherosclerotic calcification (&gt;1 cm from the aortic margin) 9,11</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>obscuration of the aortic knob</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>opacification of the AP window</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>deviation of mediastinal structures</td></tr><tr><td>o</td><td>esophagus or NGT to the right</td></tr><tr><td>o</td><td>trachea to the right</td></tr><tr><td>o</td><td>left main bronchus inferiorly (decreased angle from the horizontal)</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>increased thickness of the left and/or right paratracheal stripe</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>apical capping, particularly on the left</td></tr><tr><td>ii.</td><td>The patient has previous severe allergy to iodine contrast. You pre-medicate to consider CT scan</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">with contrast but it cannot occur immediately. List 3 other investigations which may assist your</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">diagnosis. List test and a problem associated with each investigation.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td></td><td>widened mediastinum: &gt;8.0-8.8 cm at the level of the aortic knob on portable anteroposterior chest</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">radiographs 9,10, although this upper limit of normal varies (may be significantly larger) depending on</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">projection, FFD and x-ray cassette positioning 15</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>double aortic contour</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>irregular aortic contour</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>inward displacement of atherosclerotic calcification (&gt;1 cm from the aortic margin) 9,11</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>obscuration of the aortic knob</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>opacification of the AP window</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>deviation of mediastinal structures</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">esophagus or NGT to the right</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">trachea to the right</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">left main bronchus inferiorly (decreased angle from the horizontal)</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>increased thickness of the left and/or right paratracheal stripe</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>apical capping, particularly on the left</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>1.</em><em> </em><em>Spinal infection, discitis, epidural abscess – must get this  plus 2 of below to get marks. Zero </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>marks if not listed </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>2.</em><em> </em><em> </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>a.</em><em> </em>Vasculitis<em> </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>b.</em><em> </em>PE<em> </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>c.</em><em> </em>Infective: <em> </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\">1. Lung: atypical pneumonia</div><div class=\"answer-line\">2. Pericardium eg TB pericarditis</div><div class=\"answer-line\">3. shingles  (pre rash)</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Answer guide  book 2 Practice SAQ Paper 2019.2</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Northern Sydney Hospitals</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":17,"title":"","stemText":"This question concerns medication reconciliation, pharmacokinetic changes in septic shock, and medications that prolong the QTc interval.","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"Define medication reconciliation (2 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List 3 ways in which medication reconciliation can be improved (3 marks)"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"Outline how 2 of the pathophysiological changes in septic shock alter the\npharmacokinetics of commonly used medications (4 marks)"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"List 5 medications from 5 different classes that prolong QTc (5 marks)\nDrug Class Example of Drug"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">The process of creating the most <em>accurate list of medications</em> by matching what the</div><div class=\"answer-line\">patient should be taking to that which is actually prescribed at each point of <em>transition of </em></div><div class=\"answer-line\"><em>care</em> (e.g. community  ED, ED  admit, hospital  discharge to home)</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">BPMH – Best Practice Medication History – need multi-source information (e.g. an</div><div class=\"answer-line\">informed patient/carer plus another source – LMO, community pharmacy etc)</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Serial review of medication at transition points (admission, discharge etc)</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Employ an ED pharmacist/hospital pharmacists/transition teams</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Staff education</div><div class=\"answer-line\"></div><div class=\"answer-line\">Encourage patient/carer empowerment regarding their medications</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><tbody><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Outline how 2 of the pathophysiological changes in septic shock alter the pharmacokinetics</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">of commonly used medications (4 marks)</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Sepsis leads to endothelial injury &amp; increased capillary permeability  interstitial</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">oedema. Further oedema from fluid boluses, cardiac &amp; renal dysfunction. Increases the</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">Vd of hydrophilic drugs</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Decreased clearance (hepatic/renal) with decreased organ perfusion  can lead to</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">increase concentration/toxicity +/- prolong  t1/2</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Critical illness can lead to low albumin states  decreased protein binding – higher</td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\">free/unbound concentrations</td></tr><tr><td>Answer guide  book 2 Practice SAQ Paper 2019.2</td><td>Northern Sydney Hospitals</td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">Answer guide  book 2 Practice SAQ Paper 2019.2</div><div class=\"answer-line\">Northern Sydney Hospitals</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":true,"answerSourceRetained":false},{"number":18,"title":"","stemText":"A 58 year-old man presents with three weeks of sore throat and hoarse voice.\nHe describes an acute deterioration in the last few days with SOB, fatigue, palpitations and impending sense of doom.\n\nHR 130\n\nBP 130/90\n\nRR 30\n\nSats 95% RA BSL 8\nTemperature 38.2 C\nPale, clammy, anxious, speaking in words only","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"Part A","prompt":"List three potential differential diagnoses? (3 marks)"},{"id":"b","label":"Part B","prompt":"List three bedside investigations and give your rationale for each (6 marks) Test Rationale"},{"id":"c","label":"Part C","prompt":"The patients GP phone through some bloods from earlier in the day.\nTSH <0.004 mIU/L (normal 0.4-4)\nT4 34 (normal 9-19)\nT3 10 (normal 2.6-6)\nState the most likely diagnosis"},{"id":"d","label":"Part D","prompt":"List 4 definitive pharmacological treatments for this condition. Give the class of drug and example (8 marks) Drug Class Example of Drug"}],"propPages":[],"questionPages":[],"answerPages":[],"modelAnswerHtml":"<div class=\"extracted-answer\"><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part A</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">A 58 year-old man presents with three weeks of sore throat and hoarse voice.</div><div class=\"answer-line\">He describes an acute deterioration in the last few days with SOB, fatigue, palpitations and</div><div class=\"answer-line\">impending sense of doom.</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part B</h5><div class=\"extracted-answer-table-wrap\"><table class=\"extracted-answer-table\"><thead><tr><th>Investigation</th><th>Justification</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>HR 130</td><td>BP 130/90</td></tr><tr><td>RR 30</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part C</h5><div class=\"answer-line\">BP 130/90</div><div class=\"answer-line\">RR 30</div></section><section class=\"answer-part\"><h5>Part D</h5><div class=\"empty-state\">The supplied marking resource did not contain extractable text for this part.</div></section></div>","answerExtractionVerified":false,"answerSourceRetained":false}]);
